  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 020: Overlay asks whether each new pattern lands in the correct place relative to earlier layers, making alignment a system-wide yield constraint.

Lesson 021: Critical-dimension control asks whether printed features have the correct width and shape, because tiny geometry errors can alter electrical behavior.

Lesson 022: Lithography is a closed control loop linking resolution, resist, overlay, CD, etch and metrology rather than a single exposure step.

Lesson 023: Multiple patterning creates denser structures by splitting one logical layer into several physical patterning steps, trading resolution for more process complexity.

Lesson 024: EUV dose, source power and stochastic defects connect pattern quality directly to scanner wafers-per-hour and effective lithography capacity.

The big idea

Yesterday we built the EUV capacity equation around scanner count and wafers per hour.

Today we add the variable that turns a fast machine into a useful production asset:

**availability — the fraction of scheduled production time when the tool is actually capable of running wafers.**

ASML itself uses the simple relationship:

**EUV capacity ≈ systems × throughput × availability.**

That means a fab can gain meaningful lithography capacity without buying another scanner if it can keep the installed fleet running more consistently.

## 1. What “availability” means physically

An EUV scanner is not one simple machine. It is a tightly coupled system containing a laser-produced-plasma light source, vacuum systems, reflective optics, reticle and wafer stages, sensors, contamination-control systems, cooling, software and many calibration loops.

If a critical subsystem is unavailable, the scanner may be unable to expose production wafers even if most of the machine is healthy.

Think of an airplane. Having the engines, wings and pilots does not help if one flight-critical system grounds the aircraft.

For a fab operator, time therefore falls into two broad buckets:

- **available time:** the scanner can run qualified production;
- **unavailable time:** maintenance, repair, calibration or another issue prevents production.

Some downtime is planned. Some is unexpected.

## 2. Planned maintenance is not wasted time

A scanner contains components whose performance changes with use. Engineers schedule maintenance, cleaning, replacement and calibration before those changes become yield problems or unexpected failures.

The goal is not “zero maintenance.”

The goal is to choose maintenance timing that maximizes reliable production over the long run.

Skipping maintenance may temporarily increase today’s uptime but create a larger failure tomorrow. This is the same logic airlines use when taking an aircraft out of service for inspection rather than waiting for something to break in flight.

A well-run fab also tries to place maintenance into lower-value windows and coordinate work so several tasks happen during the same outage rather than creating separate interruptions.

## 3. Unplanned downtime is more painful

Unexpected failures are worse because they interrupt the production schedule and create uncertainty.

Two useful reliability concepts are:

- **MTBF — mean time between failures:** roughly, how long the system runs before a failure occurs;
- **MTTR — mean time to repair:** roughly, how long it takes to diagnose and restore the system after a failure.

Availability improves when failures happen less often, repairs happen faster, or both.

This is why diagnostics, spare parts, field engineers, remote monitoring and predictive maintenance can become capacity tools rather than merely support functions.

